"[2209.14881] Sequential Attention for Feature Selection (https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.14881)\nciteturn26007view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn25986view1\",\"id\":37}); Total lines: 169\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Machine Learning\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 29 Sep 2022 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 25 Apr 2023 (this version, v3)]\nL9: # Title:Sequential Attention for Feature Selection\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Taisuke Yasuda , cite7†MohammadHossein Bateni , cite8†Lin Chen , cite9†Matthew Fahrbach , cite10†Gang Fu , cite11†Vahab Mirrokni L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Sequential Attention for Feature Selection, by Taisuke Yasuda and 5 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite12†View PDF cite13†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Feature selection is the problem of selecting a subset of features for a machine learning model that maximizes model quality subject to a budget constraint. For neural networks, prior methods, including those based on $\\ell_1$ regularization, attention, and other techniques, typically select the entire feature subset in one evaluation round, ignoring the residual value of features during selection, i.e., the marginal contribution of a feature given that other features have already been selected.\nL17: We propose a feature selection algorithm called Sequential Attention that achieves state-of-the-art empirical results for neural networks. This algorithm is based on an efficient one-pass implementation of greedy forward selection and uses attention weights at each step as a proxy for feature importance.\nL18: We give theoretical insights into our algorithm for linear regression by showing that an adaptation to this setting is equivalent to the classical Orthogonal Matching Pursuit (OMP) algorithm, and thus inherits all of its provable guarantees. Our theoretical and empirical analyses offer new explanations towards the effectiveness of attention and its connections to overparameterization, which may be of independent interest.\nL19: Comments:  | Accepted to ICLR 2023\nL20: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG); Machine Learning (stat.ML)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite14†arXiv:2209.14881 [cs.LG]\nL22:    | (or cite15†arXiv:2209.14881v3 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL23:    | cite16†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2209.14881†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: Journal reference:  | Proceedings of the 11th International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR 2023)\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Taisuke Yasuda [cite17†view email ]\nL28: cite5†[v1] Thu, 29 Sep 2022 15:49:06 UTC (449 KB)\nL29: cite18†[v2] Thu, 2 Mar 2023 03:23:35 UTC (495 KB)\nL30: [v3] Tue, 25 Apr 2023 15:48:02 UTC (495 KB)\nL31: \nL32: Full-text links:\nL33: \nL34: ## Access Paper:\nL35: \nL36: View a PDF of the paper titled Sequential Attention for Feature Selection, by Taisuke Yasuda and 5 other authors\nL37: \nL38:   * cite12†View PDF L39:   * cite13†HTML (experimental) L40:   * cite19†TeX Source L41: \nL42: cite20†view license†creativecommons.org L43: ### Current browse context:\nL44: \nL45: cs.LG\nL46: \nL47: cite21†< prev |   cite22†next > L48: \nL49: cite23†new | cite24†recent | cite25†2022-09 L50: \nL51: Change to browse by:\nL52: \nL53: cite26†cs L54: cite27†stat L55: cite28†stat.ML L56: \nL57: ### References & Citations\nL58: \nL59:   * cite29†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L60:   * cite30†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L61:   * cite31†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L62: \nL63: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL64: \nL65: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL66: \nL67: [Button: ×]\nL68: \nL69: loading...\nL70: