0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Machine Learning\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 27 Feb 2024 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 10 Feb 2025 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:SequentialAttention++ for Block Sparsification: Differentiable Pruning Meets Combinatorial Optimization\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Taisuke Yasuda , cite7†Kyriakos Axiotis , cite8†Gang Fu , cite9†MohammadHossein Bateni , cite10†Vahab Mirrokni L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled SequentialAttention++ for Block Sparsification: Differentiable Pruning Meets Combinatorial Optimization, by Taisuke Yasuda and 4 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite11†View PDF cite12†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Neural network pruning is a key technique towards engineering large yet scalable, interpretable, and generalizable models. Prior work on the subject has developed largely along two orthogonal directions: (1) differentiable pruning for efficiently and accurately scoring the importance of parameters, and (2) combinatorial optimization for efficiently searching over the space of sparse models.\nL17: We unite the two approaches, both theoretically and empirically, to produce a coherent framework for structured neural network pruning in which differentiable pruning guides combinatorial optimization algorithms to select the most important sparse set of parameters.\nL18: Theoretically, we show how many existing differentiable pruning techniques can be understood as nonconvex regularization for group sparse optimization, and prove that for a wide class of nonconvex regularizers, the global optimum is unique, group-sparse, and provably yields an approximate solution to a sparse convex optimization problem.\nL19: The resulting algorithm that we propose, SequentialAttention++, advances the state of the art in large-scale neural network block-wise pruning tasks on the ImageNet and Criteo datasets.\nL20: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite13†arXiv:2402.17902 [cs.LG]\nL22:    | (or cite14†arXiv:2402.17902v2 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL23:    | cite15†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2402.17902†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Kyriakos Axiotis [cite16†view email ]\nL27: cite5†[v1] Tue, 27 Feb 2024 21:42:18 UTC (198 KB)\nL28: [v2] Mon, 10 Feb 2025 22:23:56 UTC (214 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled SequentialAttention++ for Block Sparsification: Differentiable Pruning Meets Combinatorial Optimization, by Taisuke Yasuda and 4 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * cite11†View PDF L37:   * cite12†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite17†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite18†view license L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.LG\nL44: \nL45: cite19†< prev |   cite20†next > L46: \nL47: cite21†new | cite22†recent | cite23†2024-02 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite24†cs L52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite25†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L56:   * cite26†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L57:   * cite27†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L58: \nL59: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL60: \nL61: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL62: \nL63: [Button: ×]\nL64: \nL65: loading...\nL66: \nL67: Data provided by:\nL68: \nL69: ### Bookmark\nL70: \nL71: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL72: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL73: \nL74: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL75: \nL76: Bibliographic Explorer (cite28†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL77: \nL78: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL79: \nL80: Connected Papers (cite29†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com )\nL81: \nL82: [Input] Litmaps Togg